{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner complete changed-byte assessment of R3 revision4 at273125f267a6ad2ba8a5cc646cb33de626c486d7. I read all705lines/64,527bytes of the main-note diff against the previously fully read revision3, and all90lines of corrections.md. Main1,554lines/111,384bytes SHA256974242eeef94891bb779e7dc64ca18c9d6f796f6ac4a16c5e296c0a7b3179a4c; matrix19,314bytes SHA256af105daf5bbe381f94d459a81f955d8d3efb98bc09aece6d379eb6c686856974. The723-line/84,111byte facts are byte-identical, SHA25612db56d1ba4a9681e47408ebfc7cbfa75495163592a3e54bed4b85f71e76f8e0. I also reread current observation/clocks, effect-ledger, R2assembly and pin/release rules. Two-file whitespace passed. OriginalR3 request/promise were freshly inspected effective, non-stale and promised. No source, tests, installs, runtime, provider effect or deployment was executed.\n\nThe five def9c326 groups have substantial concrete repairs: report/integration sources are separated, hold-ended reuse does no new copy/mint, post-dispatch refusal retains duties, never-reused root numbers and forward-only retirement protect against same-value late deletion, founding has numbered unique-name attempts with unknown duties and no exactly-once claim, missing configured register actually fences founding, fence outcomes/receipts follow either real winner, and private export uses an inaccessible transfer stage while streams have a stored end time. The bounded unknown empty-repository duties do not silently become a second branch writer. Late check outcomeR2-11 has an explicit proposed agreement under current qualified signer/job/tree/config/no-merge conditions. None is detailed adopted architecture. Four groups remain, including a product decision for U7.\n\n1. Preserve a direct one-person change without a mandatory extra issue. U7 honestly exposes the gap, but its recommended all-issue source adds an issue commitment/hold for every change and leaves a member with no issue commitment unable to create a change. That is not the previously retained one-person no-goal/no-plan/no-selected-report path. Planner direction: the declared change form should support its own integration work through the same reusable work/commitment/hold capability, or a separately explicit composable work scope attached to the change, without requiring the person to file an unrelated issue. Prefer sharing the existing declared work capability over inventing another bespoke runtime; R2/R3 owners must propose the precise form/authority decision, reconcile counts/fields/acts/tasks/private-work UI, and return it for reviewed adoption. This is permission to revise design, not to commission source or weaken hold/grant checks. Normal contributions from issue lanes and existing split/assembly remain supported. A person can still explicitly work in an issue; it is not a universal prerequisite. The source field and its authenticated lane/hold/instance/provenance stay explicit; the page cannot claim no extra work when an undeclared prerequisite remains.\n\nAlso reconcile section6.2's last no-hold r+1 exception with the new-staging table, section5.2 and race table. Creating r+1 from closure already in the canonical repository still creates a new canonical ref and mints a staging token; it is not reuse with no effect. Either require a new held hold for that operation, or define a separately authorized root-retention operation with its own exact guards/ledger/fence. Do not let existing object bytes silently grant a former performer or an ended hold a new write.\n\n2. Finish the manifest-pin message lifecycle in both orders. P's admitted manifest sends pin-confirm; the entry that supersedes or publishes it can then send unpin. R1 permits different requests to arrive in either order. Current text allows only P's unpin to release a held pin, and only terminal-absence settlement to release a provisional one. If unpin arrives first while provisional, the manifest really was admitted, so terminal absence cannot later release it; a refused unpin followed by pin-confirm can strand the pin, or a naive late confirmation can resurrect an already released pin. Specify exact source/intent/root/pin correlation, all pin states, deduplication and terminal handling for confirm-before-unpin and unpin-before-confirm. An authenticated later release may consume/close a provisional pin without claiming the manifest was never admitted; its exact authoritative subject/evidence must show it no longer needs the root. A subsequent older confirmation must not restore it. Preserve real unresolved merges/pins and replay retention. State that a creating root's retention duty protects creation, while a consumer pin only makes a live root usable; the current 'provisional with the root' wording must not bypass the stated live-root pin guard. Enumerate these actual messages/forms with R1/R2 rather than pretending they are already in the11-item/40-act definition. One two-order table and lost-answer case suffice; no host sweep or whole-platform gate.\n\n3. Define first repository/fork selection and late successful creation. Example: founding attempt1 is unknown, attempt2 is answered created and selected, then attempt1's own authenticated created answer arrives before or after activation. Both rows currently say created records the repository and sends found/goes on, while prose says the claim names only one repository. Give the local guarded first-selection rule and the exact late-answer outcome: settle only that attempt, record the nonselected authenticated repository/returned credential duty, never replace the claim's fixed selected repository, resend found for another repository, or change the active directory. Cleanup must touch only authenticated owned IDs under the declared rules, not an unrelated occupied name. Apply the same rule to fork creation for a hold: a late answer cannot replace the fork the environment currently uses. Correlate duplicate own replies and all three bounded attempts; the next attempt remains a distinct duty and does not settle the first. The bound and retained ledger permit progress but do not supply this selection guard by themselves. Do not restore an exactly-once claim or delay unrelated work.\n\n4. Separate export release intent, actual visibility and stream time assumptions. The export correctly records release before attempting it and says a crash then leaves unknown. But the race table still says after release is recorded the bytes are delivered, contradicting that rule. Name intent/dispatched/confirmed/unknown explicitly: a recorded intent does not establish delivery, and an unknown release preserves both source/recipient pins, the exact hold/instance and its cleanup/settlement duty. Decide where authorization becomes irreversible. A fresh check followed by an intent record and an asynchronous storage release does not by itself bound actual byte visibility to ten seconds: runtime delay, restart or a pending external request can make bytes visible later, including after authority/holder changes. Either specify an actual fenced inaccessible-stage-to-recipient release operation that enforces the chosen current target/authority boundary, or disclose authorized dispatch as the irreversible point and the pending delivery it permits. Recheck before new dispatch where required; never replay an unknown release as if absent, and never claim a later revocation recalls delivered bytes. This needs no invented distributed atomicity or provider capability assertion.\n\nThe stream's10+60+10 composition now has an honest pending-check cutoff. Qualify its80-second real elapsed claim with the clock/timer assumptions that enforce it, including backward-clock/fail-closed behavior of an existing stream, not only a new grant commit. Section3.3 refuses new observations when the clock goes behind, but a stored end timestamp alone does not stop already allowed stream bytes on a regressing clock. Use the owned deadline mechanism or name the necessary operational timing assumption and retained evidence; R4 measures the exact boundary. Public10-minute sessions, private direct reads, tool authority and delivered bytes remain separate. No numerical test quota or fixed Jam gate is added.\n\nR162e51f0 and R2current2480b7b7 remain related inputs: refusal versus settlement, trustworthy journals and complete publication fanout cannot be contradicted by this note. R1 must independently resolve preparation/pin-entry rules and the register/directory confirmer; R2 must enumerate sources/new pin messages, all six issue handlers and exact forms/bounds; R3 still owes R2-12. R4 must fold these actual witnesses into its compact useful-invariant/cost plan, not multiply gates. Complete browser/durable/device, split/join/qualified review, cold-person/public-package/manual/replay/deployed and all-component retirement acceptance remains unchanged, with formal promise reconciliation and actual effect duties separately owned. Internal changes requested under original live R3 request/promise, not independent approval, source adoption, filing permission, compatibility support or provider action.","body":{"head":"273125f267a6ad2ba8a5cc646cb33de626c486d7","scope":"R3 revision4 complete changed-byte assessment and U7 product direction; design only","verdict":"internal-changes-requested"}}